WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.900
Hi. Before you get into the reading material for this case study, here's a super quick introduction to help you navigate Sunnyside Up.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:18.900
Sunnyside Up is a dummy product and an independent solar viability check for UK homes, covering both rooftops and plug-in solar.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:26.900
The UK just had its biggest year yet for solar installation, up thirty-one to thirty-seven percent in twenty-twenty five.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.900
Every comparison site in the country runs on lead generation or a referral fee.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:44.900
Households, however, are very wary of quotes that can differ by thousands of pounds with no real explanation, and renters have it even harder since they can't put anything on a roof they don't own.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.900
Most renters don't even know that a plug-in solar option exists for them, and it can cost as little as a few hundred pounds, less than most people would have guessed.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:03.900
With this case study, I have focused on establishing viability of most of the features necessary for a product like Sunnyside Up to exist.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:19.900
First was the ability to fetch live data directly from three sources: a postcode's region and coordinates from postcodes.io, solar irradiance from Open Meteo, and current electricity prices from Octopus Energy's API.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:29.900
Next one was a viability engine that covers both rooftops and plug-in solar, scoring a home green, amber, or red.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.900
And the last is every assumption behind the score that gets shown to the user right in the results.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.900
As you go through this case study, you can dive directly into Sunnyside Up's competition, personas, and research, as well as the roadmap.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:55.285
Feel free to click around with the prototype, check the solar viability for your own home as well.
